'''The Music''' were an English [[alternative rock]] band, formed in [[Kippax, West Yorkshire|Kippax]], Leeds in 1999. Comprising [[Robert Harvey (musician)|Robert Harvey]] (vocals, guitar), Adam Nutter (lead guitar), Stuart Coleman (bass) and Phil Jordan (drums), the band came to prominence with the release of their [[The Music (album)|self-titled debut album]] in 2002. The band released two further studio albums, ''[[Welcome to the North]]'' (2004) and ''[[Strength in Numbers (The Music album)|Strength in Numbers]]'' (2008), before parting ways in 2011.<ref>[http://www.themusic.co.uk/ The Music] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070814140504/http://www.themusic.co.uk/ |date=14 August 2007 }}. The Music (23 April 2011). Retrieved on 10 April 2012.</ref>

{{short description|Dead particulate organic material}}
{{other uses}}
[[File:Horse Manure and Hay Detritus.jpg|thumb|[[Horse]] [[feces]] and [[straw]] are forms of detritus, and are used as [[manure]].]]

In [[biology]], '''detritus''' ({{IPAc-en|d|ə|ˈ|t|r|aɪ|t|ə|s}} or {{IPAc-en|ˈ|d|ɛ|t|r|ɪ|t|ə|s}}) is [[organic matter]] made up of the [[decomposition|decomposing]] remains of [[organism]]s and plant material, and also of [[feces]]. Detritus usually hosts communities of [[microorganism]]s that colonize and decompose ([[Remineralisation|remineralise]]) it. Such microorganisms may be [[decomposer]]s, [[detritivore]]s, or [[coprophage]]s.

In terrestrial [[ecosystem]]s detritus is present as [[plant litter]] and other organic matter that is intermixed with [[soil]], known as [[soil organic matter]]. The detritus of [[freshwater ecosystem]]s are mostly leaf litter and other decaying organic materials. These are decomposed by microbes, and small invertebrates that shred leaves or filter feed suspended organic matter.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Cummins |first=Kenneth W. |date=1974-11-01 |title=Structure and Function of Stream Ecosystems |url=https://academic.oup.com/bioscience/article-lookup/doi/10.2307/1296676 |journal=BioScience |volume=24 |issue=11 |pages=631–641 |doi=10.2307/1296676 |jstor=1296676 |url-access=subscription }}</ref> The detritus of [[marine ecosystem]]s are organic substances suspended in the [[water]] and accumulated in depositions on the floor of the [[body of water]]; when this floor is a [[seabed]], such a deposition is called [[marine snow]].

