The '''''Geography''''' ({{langx|grc|Γεωγραφικὴ Ὑφήγησις}}, {{transliteration|grc|Geōgraphikḕ Hyphḗgēsis}}, {{abbr|lit.|Literally}}&nbsp;"Geographical Guidance"), also known by its [[Latin]] names as the '''''{{lang|la|Geographia}}''''' and the '''''{{lang|la|Cosmographia}}''''', is a [[gazetteer]], an [[atlas (book)|atlas]], and a treatise on [[cartography]], compiling the geographical knowledge of the 2nd-century [[Roman Empire]]. Originally written by [[Claudius Ptolemy]] in [[Ancient Greek|Greek]] in [[Alexandria]] around 150&nbsp;AD, the work was a revision of a now-lost atlas by [[Marinus of Tyre]] using additional Roman and [[Parthian Empire|Persian]] gazetteers and new principles.{{sfnp|Berggren|2000}}

Its translation into [[Classical Arabic|Arabic]] by [[Al-Khwarizmi|al-Khwarismi]] in the 9th century was highly influential on the [[Geography and cartography in medieval Islam|geographical knowledge and cartographic traditions of the Islamic world]]. Alongside the works of Islamic scholars—and the commentary containing revised and more accurate data by [[Alfraganus]]—Ptolemy's work was subsequently highly influential on [[Middle Ages|Medieval]] and [[Renaissance]] Europe.

